Background
----------
The AcornSSL module provides a BSD-like socket interface that allows
communication through the TCP/IP stack to resources requiring a secure
connection.

It uses the mbedTLS library for the majority of its implementation, the module
merely calls the corresponding functions and wraps them into a set of SWIs.
At the time of writing this supports all current SSL and TLS standards:

  TLS version 1.0
  TLS version 1.1
  TLS version 1.2

The earlier SSL version 2 and 3 standards are now deemed too insecure and are
not supported at all, while TLS version 1.0 is likely to be withdrawn. The 
client SWI interface offers no mechanism to select which is used, so 
attempting to connect to a server which uses one of the insecure methods
will fail.

SWIs
----
AcornSSL_Creat                                                 SWI &50F80

Initialises a secure session with a new socket.

On entry
   R0 = bits 0-7  : domain
        bits 8-31 : reserved for future use (zero)
   R1 = type
   R2 = protocol

On exit
   R0 = ssl handle

Use
   Analogous to Socket_Creat, this SWI initialises a new secure session
   returning a handle to use with the other SWIs. Note that the handle is
   not a socket handle and must be treated as opaque.
   If a socket handle (from Socket_Creat) has already been opened and
   needs to be upgraded to a secure session, see AcornSSL_CreateSession.

AcornSSL_Ioctl                                                 SWI &50F81

Perform miscellaneous socket input/output control.

On entry
   R0 = ssl handle
   R1 = operation
   R2 = pointer to argument

On exit
   R0 = 0 for success
   
Use
   Analogous to Socket_Ioctl.
   
AcornSSL_Connect                                               SWI &50F82

Connects the secure session to a remote host.

On entry
   R0 = ssl handle
   R1 = pointer to address structure
   R2 = size of address structure

On exit
   R0 = 0 for success
   
Use
   Analogous to Socket_Connect.

AcornSSL_Shutdown                                              SWI &50F83

Shut one or both halves of a connection.

On entry
   R0 = ssl handle
   R1 = direction of shutdown

On exit
   R0 = 0 for success
   
Use
   Analogous to Socket_Shutdown.

AcornSSL_Close                                                 SWI &50F84

Close a connection.

On entry
   R0 = ssl handle

On exit
   R0 = 0 for success
   
Use
   Analogous to Socket_Close.

AcornSSL_Getsockopt                                            SWI &50F85

Get a socket option.

On entry
   R0 = ssl handle
   R1 = option level
   R2 = option
   R3 = pointer to buffer for option value
   R4 = size of option value buffer

On exit
   R0 = 0 for success
   
Use
   Analogous to Socket_Getsockopt.

AcornSSL_Write                                                 SWI &50F86

Write data to a secure connection.

On entry
   R0 = ssl handle
   R1 = pointer to data to send
   R2 = amount of data to send

On exit
   R0 = amount of data written

Use
   Analogous to Socket_Write, equivalent to AcornSSL_Send with flags of 0.
   See AcornSSL_Send.

AcornSSL_Recv                                                  SWI &50F87

Read data from a secure connection.

On entry
   R0 = ssl handle
   R1 = pointer to data to receive
   R2 = size of data buffer
   R3 = option flags, only MSG_PEEK is significant

On exit
   R0 = amount of data received

Use
   Analogous to Socket_Recv.
   While establishing a secure link, and performing a certificate exchange,
   potentially requiring user confirmation, the link status may be reported
   as ENOTCONN in order to match the response the Internet module would
   give prior to a link being up.

AcornSSL_CreateSession                                         SWI &50F88

Initialises a secure session with an existing socket.

On entry
   R0 = socket handle from Socket_Creat
   R1 = bits 0-7  : reason code 0, create
        bits 8-31 : reserved for future use (zero)

On exit
   R0 = ssl handle

Use
   This SWI performs a similar function to AcornSSL_Creat, but allows the
   caller to handover a previously opened socket.

AcornSSL_Getpeername                                           SWI &50F89

Get details of the remote host's address.

On entry
   R0 = ssl handle
   R1 = pointer to address structure
   R2 = pointer to size of address block

On exit
   R0 = 0 for success

Use
   Analogous to Socket_Getpeername.

AcornSSL_Getsockname                                           SWI &50F8A

Get details of the local host's address.

On entry
   R0 = ssl handle
   R1 = pointer to address structure
   R2 = pointer to size of address block

On exit
   R0 = 0 for success

Use
   Analogous to Socket_Getsockname.

AcornSSL_Setsockopt                                            SWI &50F8B

Set a socket option.

On entry
   R0 = ssl handle
   R1 = option level
   R2 = option
   R3 = pointer to buffer for option value
   R4 = size of option value buffer

On exit
   R0 = 0 for success
   
Use
   Analogous to Socket_Setsockopt.

AcornSSL_Stat                                                  SWI &50F8C

Read status information for a socket.

On entry
   R0 = ssl handle
   R1 = pointer to stat structure

On exit
   R0 = 0 for success
   
Use
   Analogous to Socket_Stat.

AcornSSL_Version                                               SWI &50F8D

Read the module's version number.

On exit
   R0 = 100 * the version
   
Use
   Analogous to Socket_Version.
   If extra features are added in the future, the version number can be read
   to determine whether the loaded version is able to support those features.

AcornSSL_Read                                                  SWI &50F8E

Read data from a secure connection.

On entry
   R0 = ssl handle
   R1 = pointer to data to receive
   R2 = size of data buffer

On exit
   R0 = amount of data received

Use
   Analogous to Socket_Read, equivalent to AcornSSL_Recv with flags of 0.
   See AcornSSL_Recv.

AcornSSL_Send                                                  SWI &50F8F

Write data to a secure connection.

On entry
   R0 = ssl handle
   R1 = pointer to data to send
   R2 = amount of data to send
   R3 = option flags, none are significant

On exit
   R0 = amount of data written

Use
   Analogous to Socket_Send.
   While establishing a secure link, and performing a certificate exchange,
   potentially requiring user confirmation, the link status may be reported
   as ENOTCONN in order to match the response the Internet module would
   give prior to a link being up.

Commands
--------
No star commands are offered.

Service calls
-------------
Service_URLModule (&83E00)

This service call is issued by the AcornSSL module to communicate important
events.

SSL module became available

On entry:
   R0 = 0 (Service_URLModule_SSL_Started)
   R1 = &83E02 (Service_URLModule_SSL)
   R2 = version number * 100

On exit:
   All registers must be preserved. Do not claim this service call.

SSL module no longer available

On entry:
   R0 = 1 (Service_URLModule_SSL_Dying)
   R1 = &83E02 (Service_URLModule_SSL)
   R2 = version number * 100

On exit:
   All registers must be preserved. Do not claim this service call.

Extension socket options
------------------------
A session can be configured by calling the Getsockopt or Setsockopt SWIs
(after Creat or CreateSession, and before Connect) to alter parameters
relating to this specific session.

Defined options are:

SO_ACORNSSL_HOSTNAME (&11E0)
   Use
      Associates the expected name of the host with the current session.
      Because name resolution is done by the client, and a peer can present
      several alias names, there would otherwise be no way to cross check
      from only the IP address whether the certificate is issued to the
      expected name.
      The expected name of the host will also be needed if the administrator
      has enabled SNI (server name identification). Without this no name is
      sent and it is likely the connection will be refused.
      A name pointer of NULL (the default if this SWI is not called) skips
      checking the peer's name and does not send the expected name during
      handshaking.
   Set
      optval = pointer to the expected name of the host name
      optlen = 4, the size of a string pointer
   Get
      optval = pointer to receive the address of the host name into
      optlen = pointer to an integer of value 4, the size of a string pointer

SO_ACORNSSL_PROMPTTIME (&11E1)
   Use
      Defines the amount of time the prompt will wait for if a certificate
      is flagged as bad.
      A value of zero will display no prompt and immediately fail the
      connection. 
   Set
      optval = pointer to value in centiseconds
      optlen = 4, the size of an integer
   Get
      optval = pointer to receive the integer into
      optlen = pointer to an integer of value 4, the size of an integer pointer

Loading the module
------------------
Application authors wanting to ensure the AcornSSL SWIs are available should
use RMEnsure in the usual manner. For example:

   RMEnsure AcornSSL 1.23 RMLoad System:Modules.Network.URL.AcornSSL
   RMEnsure AcornSSL 1.23 Error Application requires AcornSSL 1.23 or later

A more comprehensive series of RMEnsures would be needed if an earlier AcornSSL
is loaded and has clients active; loading a second copy would end those
sessions. In order to track security enhancements it can be assumed that the
latest version is always better, even at the expense of terminating sessions.

Note the use of System: path variable. In future the module may be made
available with optimisations tailored for different versions of RISC OS, do
not refer to specific versions inside !System for this reason.

Starting a session
------------------
Which SWIs are used to get a secure connection depends on whether an insecure
dialogue is needed prior to handshaking or not. Some protocols require an
initial exchange to decide what to do before going secure. Since the SWIs
provided by AcornSSL map closely to the socket SWIs that the Internet module
provides it is convenient to look at them side by side:

                                                    Commentary
                                                    ----------
  AcornSSL_Creat          Socket_Creat              Get a handle
  AcornSSL_Connect        Socket_Connect            Connect to the remote host
  :                       :
  :                       [insecure dialogue]       Reads & writes
  :                       :
  :                       AcornSSL_CreateSession    Associate the handle
  :                       :
  [secure dialogue]       [secure dialogue]         Reads & writes
  :                       :
  AcornSSL_Close          AcornSSL_Close            Finish being secure
                          Socket_Close              Close the socket

Blocking versus non blocking
----------------------------
* Select
  The semantics of select() require a set of bitmaps with bits relating to
  each socket number to select on. However, ssl handles are opaque values
  which don't contain small incrementing integers, so the macros FD_SET,
  FD_CLR, and FD_ISSET can't be used.

  If the structure of your application requires select() then the
  recommendation is to use Socket_Creat to create the underlying socket
  yourself then AcornSSL_CreateSession to associate the socket number with
  and ssl handle. Since Socket_Create does return small incrementing integer
  socket numbers these can be used with select() as normal.

  Beware that a successful select() doesn't necessarily mean there is
  application data available. Data apparently ready for reading or writing
  on the socket may be caused by the security library handling an alert or
  updating a session ticket. Therefore a successful select() could still
  result in recv() or send() returning nothing.

* FIONBIO command
  Use AcornSSL_Ioctl to mark the ssl handle as non blocking by applying the
  command FIONBIO with a pointer to an integer 1 (to enable non blocking) or 0
  (to disable).
  
  Note that if this is done before connecting, the connect operation will also
  be non blocking and will return EINPROGRESS. As noted above it isn't
  possible to select on an ssl handle directly, so it isn't convenient to
  find out when the connection became ready for writing. The simplest
  workaround for this is to leave the connection as default (blocking) and
  then use FIONBIO before reads and writes are used. Alternatively, proceed
  anyway and expect ENOTCONN from all SWIs that need a connection: recv(),
  send(), read(), write() and getpeername().

Error reporting
---------------
The AcornSSL SWIs can return errors, some of which may imply a retry is
worth considering (EINPROGRESS being one example), others fatal. These fall
into 3 categories:
* Propagated errors
  Some component AcornSSL relies on has thrown an error, and you receive this,
  for example a 'File not found' error when AcornSSL wants to read a file.
* Unix errors
  For maximum compatibility with the BSD sockets API, most errors returned
  will be mapped to Unix errors in the DCI4 error range. For a full list of
  these see 
    C:TCPIPLibs.sys.h.errno
  and for macros to recognise and extract them from the RISC OS error see
    C:TCPIPLibs.sys.h.dcistructs
* Specific errors
  Errors in the range &813F20-813F3F are allocated to AcornSSL. These errors
  are ones that are specific to the module but can't reasonably be given
  a Unix error, plus any errors from SWIs that are unrelated to a BSD 
  sockets API related call (AcornSSL_CreateSession being one example).
